arXiv:2509.15556v1 [cs.CL] 19 Sep 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Exploring Polyglot Harmony: On Multilingual Data Allocation for Large Language Models Pretraining</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Ping Guo
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>ByteDance
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yubing Ren
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Institute of Information Engineering, Chinese Academy of Sciences
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Binbin Liu
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>ByteDance
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Fengze Liu
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>ByteDance
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Haobin Lin  Yifan Zhang  Bingni Zhang  Taifeng Wang  Yin Zheng
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>ByteDance
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) have become integral to a wide range of applications worldwide, driving an unprecedented global demand for effective multilingual capabilities. Central to achieving robust multilingual performance is the strategic allocation of language proportions within training corpora. However, determining optimal language ratios is highly challenging due to intricate cross-lingual interactions and sensitivity to dataset scale. This paper introduces <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Climb</span> (<span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">C</span>ross-<span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">L</span>ingual <span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">I</span>nteraction-aware <span id="abstract1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">M</span>ultilingual <span id="abstract1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">B</span>alancing), a novel framework designed to systematically optimize multilingual data allocation. At its core, <span id="abstract1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Climb</span> introduces a <span id="abstract1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cross-lingual interaction-aware language ratio</span>, explicitly quantifying each language’s effective allocation by capturing inter-language dependencies. Leveraging this ratio, <span id="abstract1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Climb</span> proposes a principled two-step optimization procedure—first equalizing marginal benefits across languages, then maximizing the magnitude of the resulting language allocation vectors—significantly simplifying the inherently complex multilingual optimization problem. Extensive experiments confirm that <span id="abstract1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Climb</span> can accurately measure cross-lingual interactions across various multilingual settings. LLMs trained with <span id="abstract1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Climb</span>-derived proportions consistently achieve state-of-the-art multilingual performance, even achieve competitive performance with open-sourced LLMs trained with more tokens.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs), exemplified by the GPT series <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>)</cite>, LLaMA series <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>, <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>)</cite>, Gemma series <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>)</cite>, Qwen series <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>)</cite>, and DeepSeek series <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>)</cite>, have reshaped various language-based applications worldwide, powering advanced chatbots <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, machine translation systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>, and intelligent virtual assistants <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite>. Such impressive capabilities emerge predominantly from extensive pretraining on enormous textual datasets, frequently spanning tens to hundreds of trillions of tokens, enabling the capture of rich and diverse linguistic knowledge. Driven by the growing global demand and the need for equitable language representation, there has been an accelerating shift toward multilingual pretraining, aiming to transcend linguistic boundaries and serve a broader range of linguistic communities effectively <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>]</cite>. Central to this shift lies a fundamental question: <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">how should the proportions of different languages be optimally allocated within the training corpus to achieve balanced and superior model performance across all target languages?</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, determining an optimal multilingual mixture poses considerable challenges. The foremost difficulty arises from cross-lingual interactions: performance on one language can be significantly influenced by other languages trained concurrently <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Exploring Polyglot Harmony: On Multilingual Data Allocation for Large Language Models Pretraining" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, even when the training proportion of Arabic remains fixed to 10%, modifying the proportions of the other four languages (increasing one language to 60% proportion) in a five-language LLM can substantially alter Arabic’s performance. This interdependence prevents isolated optimization of individual languages and necessitates joint optimization of the entire language set. Additionally, optimal language ratios are sensitive to the scale of the training corpus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. Specifically, language proportions identified as optimal at smaller scales (e.g., 1 billion tokens) may no longer remain optimal when scaled to larger training sets (e.g., 4 trillion tokens), rendering simple extrapolations unreliable and incurring prohibitive experimental costs. Consequently, current multilingual LLMs often resort to heuristic trial-and-error approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>)</cite>, or reuse language ratios derived from prior models without systematic justification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>)</cite>, highlighting a critical need for a principled and scalable solution to multilingual data allocation.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:39%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15556v1/ar_0.1_res.svg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:181/120;" width="181" height="120"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Cross-lingual Interactions in a five-langauge LLM.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In pursuit of achieving the optimal language allocation, this paper explores whether it is possible to accurately predict model performance under various language allocations without explicitly training the models. Inspired by the concept of scaling laws, which characterize how a model’s validation loss systematically scales with model size (<math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>) and data volume (<math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>)</cite>, we hypothesize that a similar predictive framework could be applied to multilingual settings by incorporating language proportions. Specifically, if we can formulate a mathematical relationship that captures how validation performance varies with language proportions in the training corpus, then it becomes feasible to infer optimal language ratios by identifying the allocations that minimize validation loss. However, due to the intricate cross-lingual interactions among languages, precisely modeling and predicting validation performance across different language compositions remains highly challenging.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we propose <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">Climb<span id="S1.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> (Cross-Lingual Interaction-aware Multilingual Balancing)</span></span>, a novel framework designed to systematically optimize language proportions for multilingual LLM pre-training. Our approach consists of two interconnected components. First, we introduce the <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cross-lingual interaction-aware language ratio</span>, a novel metric that explicitly quantifies the effective allocation of each language in the presence of cross-lingual interactions, effectively reflecting the impact of other jointly trained languages. Second, leveraging these cross-lingual interaction-aware ratios, we can estimate the optimal multilingual balance by decomposing the optimization into two steps: initially, we determine the direction of optimal allocation by equalizing the marginal benefits across languages; subsequently, we obtain the estimated optimal proportions by maximizing the magnitude of the resulting cross-lingual interaction-aware language ratio vector. This principled two-step procedure enables efficient and accurate computation of multilingual data distributions, significantly reducing the complexity inherent in direct joint optimization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To comprehensively evaluate the effectiveness of <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Climb</span>, we conduct experiments in two primary aspects. First, we validate the predictive accuracy of the proposed cross-lingual interaction-aware language ratio. By integrating this novel ratio into the multilingual scaling law framework, we observe a substantial improvement in predictive accuracy compared to baseline scaling laws relying on independence assumptions among languages. Second, leveraging the optimal proportions computed via <span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Climb</span>, we train multilingual LLMs at both 1.2B and 7B parameter scales. Experimental results demonstrate that models pretrained with <span id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Climb</span>-derived ratios consistently achieve state-of-the-art performance compared to various baselines with alternative language allocations. Remarkably, even compared to open-sourced models pretrained on more tokens, our <span id="S1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Climb</span>-optimized models exhibit highly competitive performance across multiple multilingual benchmarks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span><span id="S2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Climb</span></h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our approach comprises two main components: the <em id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Cross-lingual Interaction-aware Language Ratio</em>, which explicitly quantifies effective language proportions by considering cross-lingual interactions, and the <em id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Optimal Multilingual Balance</em>, which leverages these interaction-aware ratios to estimate the optimal language allocation <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> that minimizes multilingual validation loss.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Problem Formulation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a multilingual corpus consisting of training data from <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> distinct languages <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{L_{1},\dots,L_{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{L_{1},\dots,L_{m}}</annotation></semantics></math>, our objective is to determine the optimal language allocation for pretraining LLMs. Formally, we define the language proportion vector as <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}=[r_{1},r_{2},\dots,r_{m}]^{\top}\in\mathcal{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐫</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}=[r_{1},r_{2},\dots,r_{m}]^{\top}\in\mathcal{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{m}=\{\mathbf{r}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}\mid\sum_{i=1}^{m}r_{i}=1,r_{i}\geq 0,\forall i\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>𝐫</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{m}=\{\mathbf{r}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}\mid\sum_{i=1}^{m}r_{i}=1,r_{i}\geq 0,\forall i\}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the probability simplex.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S2.F2.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15556v1/ar_0.1_tilde_r_40.svg" id="S2.F2.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:143/96;" width="143" height="96"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(a)</span> </span><math id="S2.F2.sf1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{r}_{ar}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.800em">r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{r}_{ar}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.F2.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> as a function of <math id="S2.F2.sf1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{ar}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{ar}</annotation></semantics></math> with varying co-training languages</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S2.F2.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15556v1/ar_different_lang_nums.svg" id="S2.F2.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:143/95;" width="143" height="95"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(b)</span> </span><math id="S2.F2.sf2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{r}_{ar}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.800em">r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{r}_{ar}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.F2.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> as a function of <math id="S2.F2.sf2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{ar}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{ar}</annotation></semantics></math> with varying language counts</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S2.F2.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15556v1/ar_0.1_tilde_r_ko.svg" id="S2.F2.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:143/96;" width="143" height="96"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(c)</span> </span><math id="S2.F2.sf3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{r}_{ar}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.800em">r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{r}_{ar}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.F2.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> as a function of <math id="S2.F2.sf3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{ar}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{ar}</annotation></semantics></math> with varying training data scales</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Illustration of cross-lingual interaction-aware language ratio (<math id="S2.F2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{r}_{ar}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{r}_{ar}</annotation></semantics></math>) and its dependency on original training proportions (<math id="S2.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{ar}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{ar}</annotation></semantics></math>).</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given a total token budget <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, each language <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> contributes <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{i}=\lfloor r_{i}\cdot D\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{i}=\lfloor r_{i}\cdot D\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math> tokens to the training set. The model parameters <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> are trained via empirical risk minimization: <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{*}(D,\mathbf{r})=\arg\min_{\theta}\mathcal{L}(\theta;D,\mathbf{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐫</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐫</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{*}(D,\mathbf{r})=\arg\min_{\theta}\mathcal{L}(\theta;D,\mathbf{r})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\theta;D,\mathbf{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐫</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\theta;D,\mathbf{r})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the next-token prediction loss on the multilingual training set defined by proportions <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}</annotation></semantics></math> and token budget <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the pretrained model, we measure validation loss on a language-specific held-out set <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{i}^{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>v</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{i}^{v}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{i}^{v}(\theta^{*}(D,\mathbf{r}))=\mathcal{L}(\theta^{*}(D,\mathbf{r});D_{i}^{v})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐫</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐫</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{i}^{v}(\theta^{*}(D,\mathbf{r}))=\mathcal{L}(\theta^{*}(D,\mathbf{r});D_{i}^{v})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our goal is to identify the optimal language proportion vector <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> that achieves balanced multilingual performance by minimizing a weighted sum of validation losses across all languages:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\footnotesize\mathbf{r}^{*}=\arg\min_{\mathbf{r}\in\mathcal{R}^{m}}\sum_{i=1}^{m}\omega_{i}\cdot\mathcal{L}_{i}^{v}(\theta^{*}(D,\mathbf{r}))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝐫</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">∗</mo></msup><mo mathsize="0.800em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi mathsize="0.800em">min</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝐫</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.800em">ℛ</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">m</mi></msup></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">m</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">ω</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.800em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.800em">ℒ</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">v</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">θ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝐫</mi><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\footnotesize\mathbf{r}^{*}=\arg\min_{\mathbf{r}\in\mathcal{R}^{m}}\sum_{i=1}^{m}\omega_{i}\cdot\mathcal{L}_{i}^{v}(\theta^{*}(D,\mathbf{r})),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">where hyperparameter <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{i}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{i}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> specify the relative importance of each language <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, set according to application-specific requirements or practical considerations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">This formulation defines a bi-level optimization problem, in which the outer optimization seeks optimal language proportions, and the inner optimization involves training an LLM given these language proportions. Due to the intrinsic complexity of cross-lingual interactions and the prohibitive computational cost of repeated model retraining, directly solving this optimization problem through standard gradient-based approaches is computationally infeasible.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Cross-Lingual Interaction-aware Language Ratio</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a total token budget <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> and a language proportion vector <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can obtain the validation loss <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{i}^{v}(D,\mathbf{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐫</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{i}^{v}(D,\mathbf{r})</annotation></semantics></math> for language <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{D}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{D}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be the number of tokens required to reach the validation loss <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{i}^{v}(D,\mathbf{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐫</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{i}^{v}(D,\mathbf{r})</annotation></semantics></math> from a monolingual model solely on language <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we then define the <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">cross-lingual interaction-aware ratio</span> <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{r}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{r}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as the ratio of this equivalent monolingual token budget <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{D}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{D}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to the actual multilingual token budget <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>:
<math id="S2.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{r}_{i}=\frac{\tilde{D}_{i}}{D}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>D</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{r}_{i}=\frac{\tilde{D}_{i}}{D}.</annotation></semantics></math>
Formally, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{r}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{r}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> can be formally expressed as:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\footnotesize\tilde{r}_{i}=\frac{1}{D}\left(\frac{B_{i}}{\mathcal{L}_{i}^{v}(D,\mathbf{r})-E_{i}}\right)^{1/\beta_{i}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.800em">r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.800em">=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn><mi mathsize="0.800em">D</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">B</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.800em">ℒ</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">v</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">D</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝐫</mi><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">−</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">E</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">β</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\footnotesize\tilde{r}_{i}=\frac{1}{D}\left(\frac{B_{i}}{\mathcal{L}_{i}^{v}(D,\mathbf{r})-E_{i}}\right)^{1/\beta_{i}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">where parameters <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are derived from the monolingual scaling law <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>. Specifically, the monolingual scaling law characterizes how the validation loss decreases as training data volume increases for a single language <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, expressed as:</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\footnotesize\mathcal{L}_{i}^{v}(D_{i},r_{i}=1)=\frac{B_{i}}{D_{i}^{\beta_{i}}}+E_{i}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.800em">ℒ</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">v</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">D</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">r</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.800em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">=</mo><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">B</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">D</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">β</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub></msubsup></mfrac><mo mathsize="0.800em">+</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">E</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\footnotesize\mathcal{L}_{i}^{v}(D_{i},r_{i}=1)=\frac{B_{i}}{D_{i}^{\beta_{i}}}+E_{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the token budget allocated exclusively to language <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. In the absence of cross-lingual transfer, the interaction-aware ratio <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{r}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{r}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> equals the actual ratio <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, thus the difference <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{r}_{i}-r_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{r}_{i}-r_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> quantifies the magnitude of cross-lingual effects from other languages.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.2.1 </span>Empirical Observations and Insights</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To systematically understand the behavior of the cross-lingual interaction-aware language ratio <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{r}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{r}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we conducted over 300 experiments on Transformer-based models. Specifically, we varied the number of jointly trained languages (2, 3, and 5 languages), total token budgets ranging from 5 billion to 100 billion tokens, and explored a wide range of language proportion vectors <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}</annotation></semantics></math>. For each configuration, we computed the pairs <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r_{i},\tilde{r}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r_{i},\tilde{r}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> to examine how the effective language ratio deviates from the actual proportion due to cross-lingual transfer. These results are visualized in Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.1 Problem Formulation ‣ 2 Climb ‣ Exploring Polyglot Harmony: On Multilingual Data Allocation for Large Language Models Pretraining" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, from which we identify following key empirical insights:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dependency on absolute language proportion.</span> Cross-lingual transfer strength diminishes as the actual language proportion (<math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>) increases, with the slope gradually decreasing and approaching linearity at higher proportions, as illustrated in Figures <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.1 Problem Formulation ‣ 2 Climb ‣ Exploring Polyglot Harmony: On Multilingual Data Allocation for Large Language Models Pretraining" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (a), (b), and (c).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dependency on co-training languages.</span> The specific set of co-training languages affects cross-lingual transfer primarily when the language proportion <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is small, as demonstrated in Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.1 Problem Formulation ‣ 2 Climb ‣ Exploring Polyglot Harmony: On Multilingual Data Allocation for Large Language Models Pretraining" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (a). This influence diminishes as <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> grows.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dependency on model language counts.</span> Increasing the number of co-trained languages affects the intercept rather than the slope of the cross-lingual transfer relationship. This variation shifts the onset point at which transfer strength approaches linearity, as shown in Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.1 Problem Formulation ‣ 2 Climb ‣ Exploring Polyglot Harmony: On Multilingual Data Allocation for Large Language Models Pretraining" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (b).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dependency on data scale.</span> Cross-lingual transfer consistently weakens with larger total token budgets (<math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>), indicating that increased training data volume reduces dependency between languages, as depicted in Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.1 Problem Formulation ‣ 2 Climb ‣ Exploring Polyglot Harmony: On Multilingual Data Allocation for Large Language Models Pretraining" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (c).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.2.2 </span>Parametric Modeling of Cross-Lingual Interaction-aware Ratio</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Motivated by the empirical insights described above, we propose a parametric model to capture the relationship between the cross-lingual interaction-aware language ratio <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{r}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{r}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and the actual language ratio <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, we model <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{r}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{r}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as:</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\footnotesize\tilde{r}_{i}=r_{i}+\left(\sum_{j\neq i}\alpha_{j\rightarrow i}(D)\cdot r_{j}\right)\left(1-e^{-\eta_{i}r_{i}}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.800em">r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.800em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">r</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.800em">+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">≠</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">α</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">D</mi><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">r</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.800em">−</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">e</mi><mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">η</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">r</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\footnotesize\tilde{r}_{i}=r_{i}+\left(\sum_{j\neq i}\alpha_{j\rightarrow i}(D)\cdot r_{j}\right)\left(1-e^{-\eta_{i}r_{i}}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the parameters are defined as follows:</p>
<ul id="S2.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{j\rightarrow i}(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{j\rightarrow i}(D)</annotation></semantics></math> represents the transfer strength from language <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> to language <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Empirically, we find that this transfer effect diminishes linearly with increasing token budget <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, which we model as: <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{j\rightarrow i}(D)=b_{ji}+\frac{k_{ji}}{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mi>D</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{j\rightarrow i}(D)=b_{ji}+\frac{k_{ji}}{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{ji}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{ji}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates the initial strength of cross-lingual transfer from <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{ji}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{ji}</annotation></semantics></math> quantifies the rate at which this transfer strength decays as data volume increases. Details about <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{j\rightarrow i}(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{j\rightarrow i}(D)</annotation></semantics></math> is in Appendix A.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> captures the intrinsic data sufficiency of language <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. A larger value of <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates that language <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> remains reliant on cross-lingual transfer across a wider range of proportions, exhibiting a pronounced curved (transfer-dominated) regime. Conversely, a smaller <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> signals that language <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> quickly enters a linear (self-dominated) regime, reflecting sufficient self-contained data.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.2.3 </span>Complete Cross-Lingual Interaction-aware Scaling Law</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By incorporating the parametric definition of the cross-lingual interaction-aware ratio into the monolingual scaling law, we obtain our final scaling law formulation:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\footnotesize\mathcal{L}_{i}^{v}(D,\mathbf{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.800em">ℒ</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">v</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝐫</mi><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\footnotesize\mathcal{L}_{i}^{v}(D,\mathbf{r})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{B_{i}}{\left[D\cdot\tilde{r}_{i}\right]^{\beta_{i}}}+E_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{B_{i}}{\left[D\cdot\tilde{r}_{i}\right]^{\beta_{i}}}+E_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{B_{i}}{\left[D\cdot\left(r_{i}+\left(\sum_{j\neq i}(b_{ji}+\frac{k_{ji}}{D})\cdot r_{j}\right)\cdot(1-e^{-\eta_{i}r_{i}})\right)\right]^{\beta_{i}}}+E_{i}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mi>D</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{B_{i}}{\left[D\cdot\left(r_{i}+\left(\sum_{j\neq i}(b_{ji}+\frac{k_{ji}}{D})\cdot r_{j}\right)\cdot(1-e^{-\eta_{i}r_{i}})\right)\right]^{\beta_{i}}}+E_{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this equation, the complete set of parameters to estimate are: <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{B_{i},\beta_{i},E_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m},\,\{b_{ji},k_{ji}\}_{i,j=1,j\neq i}^{m},\,\{\eta_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{B_{i},\beta_{i},E_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m},\,\{b_{ji},k_{ji}\}_{i,j=1,j\neq i}^{m},\,\{\eta_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS3.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Parameter Estimation Procedure.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To fully determine these parameters, we perform targeted experiments involving each language individually. Specifically, for each language <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we conduct three experiments with distinct proportions: one monolingual scenario (where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}=1</annotation></semantics></math>) to estimate the baseline scaling law parameters <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and two additional multilingual experiments with randomly chosen language proportions <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and the remaining languages allocated equally as <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1-r_{i}}{m-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1-r_{i}}{m-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each of these experiments is repeated at two distinct training token budgets to ensure reliable parameter fitting across data scales. Following the experimental setup <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, we fit our scaling law parameters only using data points from the last 15% of training. Thus, for a setting with <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> languages, this structured approach requires a total of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times m\times 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times m\times 2</annotation></semantics></math> experiments, enabling comprehensive and accurate estimation of the proposed scaling law parameters. The detailed fitting procedure is summarized in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Parameter Estimation Procedure. ‣ 2.2.3 Complete Cross-Lingual Interaction-aware Scaling Law ‣ 2.2 Cross-Lingual Interaction-aware Language Ratio ‣ 2 Climb ‣ Exploring Polyglot Harmony: On Multilingual Data Allocation for Large Language Models Pretraining" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> <span id="alg1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Climb</span></figcaption>
<div id="alg1.6" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
                  
                  
                Languages <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{L_{1},\dots,L_{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{L_{1},\dots,L_{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, token budgets <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{D^{(1)},D^{(2)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{D^{(1)},D^{(2)}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
                  
                  
                Parameters <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{B_{i},\beta_{i},E_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m},\,\{b_{ji},k_{ji}\}_{i,j=1,j\neq i}^{m},\,\{\eta_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{B_{i},\beta_{i},E_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m},\,\{b_{ji},k_{ji}\}_{i,j=1,j\neq i}^{m},\,\{\eta_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, optimal language proportions <math id="alg1.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
                  
                  
                <span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Part I: Parameter Modeling of Cross-Lingual Interaction-aware Language Ratio</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
                  
                  
                <span id="alg1.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> each language <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
                  
                  
                  Conduct monolingual experiments (<math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}=1</annotation></semantics></math>) at <math id="alg1.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{(1)},D^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{(1)},D^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
                  
                  
                  Fit monolingual scaling law <a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2.2 Cross-Lingual Interaction-aware Language Ratio ‣ 2 Climb ‣ Exploring Polyglot Harmony: On Multilingual Data Allocation for Large Language Models Pretraining" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> to estimate <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
                  
                  
                  <span id="alg1.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> each proportion <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}=c_{i}\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}=c_{i}\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, repeat twice <span id="alg1.l7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
                  
                  
                   Set other languages proportion <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{j}=\frac{1-c_{i}}{m-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{j}=\frac{1-c_{i}}{m-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall j\neq i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall j\neq i</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
                  
                  
                   <span id="alg1.l9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> each token budget <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\in\{D^{(1)},D^{(2)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\in\{D^{(1)},D^{(2)}\}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
                  
                  
                     Train model with proportions <math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}</annotation></semantics></math> and budget <math id="alg1.l10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
                  
                  
                     Record validation loss <math id="alg1.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{i}^{v}(D,\mathbf{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐫</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{i}^{v}(D,\mathbf{r})</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
                  
                  
                     Compute <math id="alg1.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{r}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{r}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> from Eq. (<a href="#S2.E6" title="In 2.2.3 Complete Cross-Lingual Interaction-aware Scaling Law ‣ 2.2 Cross-Lingual Interaction-aware Language Ratio ‣ 2 Climb ‣ Exploring Polyglot Harmony: On Multilingual Data Allocation for Large Language Models Pretraining" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>).

</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
                  
                  
                   <span id="alg1.l13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l13.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l14.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">14:</span></span>
                  
                  
                  <span id="alg1.l14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l14.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l15" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l15.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">15:</span></span>
                  
                  
                  Fit parameters <math id="alg1.l15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{ji}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{ji}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{ji}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{ji}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> using <math id="alg1.l15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r_{i},\tilde{r}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r_{i},\tilde{r}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> pairs.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l16" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l16.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">16:</span></span>
                  
                  
                <span id="alg1.l16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l16.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l17" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l17.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">17:</span></span>
                  
                  
                <span id="alg1.l17.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Part II: Estimating Optimal Multilingual Balanced Allocation</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l18" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l18.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">18:</span></span>
                  
                  
                Compute optimal direction components <math id="alg1.l18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> via Eq. (<a href="#S2.E7" title="In 2.3.1 Optimal Direction via Marginal-Benefit Balancing. ‣ 2.3 Estimating Optimal Multilingual Balanced Allocation ‣ 2 Climb ‣ Exploring Polyglot Harmony: On Multilingual Data Allocation for Large Language Models Pretraining" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>).

</div>
<div id="alg1.l19" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l19.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">19:</span></span>
                  
                  
                Normalize direction: <math id="alg1.l19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}_{i}\leftarrow p_{i}/\sum_{j}p_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}_{i}\leftarrow p_{i}/\sum_{j}p_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="alg1.l19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l20" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l20.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">20:</span></span>
                  
                  
                Solve constrained optimization (Eq. (<a href="#S2.E8" title="In 2.3.2 Optimal Magnitude via Constrained Effective Allocation Maximization. ‣ 2.3 Estimating Optimal Multilingual Balanced Allocation ‣ 2 Climb ‣ Exploring Polyglot Harmony: On Multilingual Data Allocation for Large Language Models Pretraining" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>)).

</div>
<div id="alg1.l21" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l21.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">21:</span></span>
                  
                  
                <span id="alg1.l21.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> parameters <math id="alg1.l21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{B_{i},\beta_{i},E_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m},\,\{b_{ji},k_{ji}\}_{i,j=1,j\neq i}^{m},\,\{\eta_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{B_{i},\beta_{i},E_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m},\,\{b_{ji},k_{ji}\}_{i,j=1,j\neq i}^{m},\,\{\eta_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, and optimal proportions <math id="alg1.l21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Estimating Optimal Multilingual Balanced Allocation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Directly minimizing the multilingual validation loss defined by Equation (<a href="#S2.E6" title="In 2.2.3 Complete Cross-Lingual Interaction-aware Scaling Law ‣ 2.2 Cross-Lingual Interaction-aware Language Ratio ‣ 2 Climb ‣ Exploring Polyglot Harmony: On Multilingual Data Allocation for Large Language Models Pretraining" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) is challenging, as it forms a non-convex optimization problem in language proportions <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}</annotation></semantics></math>. While it may appear intuitive to directly optimize the cross-lingual interaction-aware language ratios <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{r}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{r}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> under Equation (<a href="#S2.E6" title="In 2.2.3 Complete Cross-Lingual Interaction-aware Scaling Law ‣ 2.2 Cross-Lingual Interaction-aware Language Ratio ‣ 2 Climb ‣ Exploring Polyglot Harmony: On Multilingual Data Allocation for Large Language Models Pretraining" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), this objective is intractable in practice, as the total sum <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i}\tilde{r}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i}\tilde{r}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> remains unknown. To address this difficulty, we propose a two-stage optimization procedure that decomposes the original complex problem into two simpler, sequential steps. Specifically, we first determine the optimal direction in the cross-lingual interaction-aware language ratios <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{r}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{r}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> space, ensuring balanced marginal benefits across languages. Subsequently, we optimize the magnitude along this determined direction to identify the final allocation <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}</annotation></semantics></math> that maximizes the overall cross-lingual interaction-aware language ratios <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{r}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{r}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, effectively minimizing the multilingual validation loss.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.3.1 </span>Optimal Direction via Marginal-Benefit Balancing.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the first stage, we identify the optimal direction for the cross-lingual interaction-aware language ratios <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{r}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{r}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> by balancing the marginal benefits across all languages. Specifically, we derive the optimal proportional relationship between the interaction-aware ratios by equalizing the marginal validation-loss reduction contributed by each language. The resulting optimal direction <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for each language <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is formally given by (see detailed derivation in Appendix B):</p>
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\footnotesize p_{i}=\frac{\left(\omega_{i}B_{i}\beta_{i}\right)^{1/(\beta_{i}+1)}D^{-\beta_{i}/(\beta_{i}+1)}}{\sum_{k=1}^{m}\left(\omega_{k}B_{k}\beta_{k}\right)^{1/(\beta_{k}+1)}D^{-\beta_{k}/(\beta_{k}+1)}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">p</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.800em">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">ω</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">β</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">β</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.800em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">D</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">−</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">β</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">β</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.800em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">m</mi></msup><mo>(</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">ω</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">k</mi></msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">k</mi></msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">β</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">k</mi></msub><mo>)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">β</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">k</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.800em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">D</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">−</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">β</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">k</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">β</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">k</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.800em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\footnotesize p_{i}=\frac{\left(\omega_{i}B_{i}\beta_{i}\right)^{1/(\beta_{i}+1)}D^{-\beta_{i}/(\beta_{i}+1)}}{\sum_{k=1}^{m}\left(\omega_{k}B_{k}\beta_{k}\right)^{1/(\beta_{k}+1)}D^{-\beta_{k}/(\beta_{k}+1)}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are the monolingual scaling-law parameters of language <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the predefined importance weight for language <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Intuitively, the direction <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates the ideal relative allocation of interaction-aware language ratios, balancing each language’s data efficiency, validation-loss reduction rate, and relative importance. Identifying this optimal direction substantially reduces complexity in subsequent optimization steps by constraining the search space for the final language proportions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.3.2 </span>Optimal Magnitude via Constrained Effective Allocation Maximization.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With the optimal direction <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> identified, the second stage focuses on determining the optimal magnitude along this direction. Nevertheless, due to the monotonicity of the scaling law function <a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2.2 Cross-Lingual Interaction-aware Language Ratio ‣ 2 Climb ‣ Exploring Polyglot Harmony: On Multilingual Data Allocation for Large Language Models Pretraining" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we find that a larger aggregate <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i}\tilde{r}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i}\tilde{r}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> consistently implies a lower overall training loss, thereby revealing an implicit preference for maximizing effective data contributions across languages (details in Appendix C). Specifically, we recover the actual language proportions <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}</annotation></semantics></math> by solving a constrained optimization problem that maximizes the total cross-lingual interaction-aware language ratio while staying close to the previously determined direction <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. Formally, this optimization objective is defined as:</p>
<table id="S2.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\footnotesize\min_{\mathbf{r}}\left[-\sum_{i=1}^{m}\hat{r}_{i}(\mathbf{r})+\rho\sum_{i=1}^{m}(\hat{r}_{i}(\mathbf{r})-p_{i})^{2}\right],\hskip 8.50012pt\text{s.t.}\hskip 8.50012pt\sum_{i=1}^{m}r_{i}=1,\hskip 8.50012ptr_{i}\geq 0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi mathsize="0.800em">min</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝐫</mi></munder><mrow><mo>[</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.800em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munderover><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">m</mi></munderover><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.800em">r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝐫</mi><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">+</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">ρ</mi><munderover><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">m</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.800em">r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝐫</mi><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">−</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">p</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow><mn mathsize="0.800em">2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em" rspace="1.017em">,</mo><mtext mathsize="0.800em">s.t.</mtext><munderover><mo lspace="1.017em" maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">m</mi></munderover><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">r</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.800em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.800em" rspace="1.017em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">r</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.800em">≥</mo><mn mathsize="0.800em">0</mn><mo mathsize="0.800em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\footnotesize\min_{\mathbf{r}}\left[-\sum_{i=1}^{m}\hat{r}_{i}(\mathbf{r})+\rho\sum_{i=1}^{m}(\hat{r}_{i}(\mathbf{r})-p_{i})^{2}\right],\hskip 8.50012pt\text{s.t.}\hskip 8.50012pt\sum_{i=1}^{m}r_{i}=1,\hskip 8.50012ptr_{i}\geq 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{r}_{i}=\frac{\tilde{r}_{i}}{\sum_{j}\tilde{r}_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub></mstyle><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{r}_{i}=\frac{\tilde{r}_{i}}{\sum_{j}\tilde{r}_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the normalized interaction-aware ratios and direction components, respectively. The first term of the objective function aims at maximizing the overall interaction-aware language ratio, corresponding directly to minimizing multilingual validation loss, while the second term (soft-constraint) penalizes deviations from the optimal direction <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. The hyperparameter <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> balances these two objectives, with smaller values of <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> emphasizing pure loss minimization and larger values enforcing adherence to the optimal direction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This problem is inherently non-convex due to the interaction-aware ratio’s nonlinearity. Thus, we adopt a Trust-Region Interior-Point Method to efficiently handle this constrained optimization problem. This structured reformulation significantly reduces the complexity and dimensionality of the original allocation problem, accelerating convergence and improving numerical stability.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Crosslingual Interaction-aware Language Ratio Evaluation</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Model Architecture.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We utilize the LLaMA-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>)</cite> architecture with 1.2 billion parameters, training all models from scratch with randomly initialized weights. All experiments are condcuted on Nvidia H100 GPU cards. To ensure consistency with established scaling-law practices, we follow the Chinchilla configuration and set a fixed number of training steps for each dataset size, adjusting the learning-rate decay schedule to cosine. Validation losses are calculated by averaging the results obtained from the final three training steps.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Datasets.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All experiments are conducted using data sampled from the Fineweb-2 corpus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>. To rigorously evaluate our Cross-Lingual Interaction-aware Language Ratio across diverse linguistic scenarios, we conduct experiments involving models trained on 2, 3, 5, and 16 languages, respectively. For each multilingual setting, we vary the token budgets from 5 billion to 100 billion tokens. The specific language compositions and detailed training procedures are documented in the Appendix D.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Fitting and Extrapolation Performance of Different Methods (<math id="S3.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}\!\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">↑</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}\!\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math> and Huber Loss<math id="S3.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math>) for Multilingual LLMs at 100B and 1T Tokens.</figcaption>
<div id="S3.T1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:81.4pt;vertical-align:-39.2pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-113.4pt,26.7pt) scale(0.603322989905338,0.603322989905338) ;">
